Found while measuring karr #56 (sops 3.13.3, Perl 5.36.0). value_to_bytes has a deliberate branch for these -- it writes +Inf, -Inf and NaN, which is what Gos strconv.FormatFloat produces -- but nothing on the emitting side can carry them, and nothing refuses them either.

Measured, an unencrypted 9**9**9 (+Inf), default configuration:

  format  backend           document says   result
  json    Cpanel::JSON::XS  null            self MAC FAIL, sops -d exit 51
  json    JSON::XS          inf             sops -d exit 1, "Could not unmarshal input data: invalid character i"
  yaml    (YAML::XS)        Inf             self decrypt OK, sops -d exit 51 (MAC mismatch)

The digest covers +Inf in all three cases. Under Cpanel the value is not merely misrendered, it is GONE -- null -- and the document is unreadable by either implementation.

JSON has no representation for these at all, so there is no rendering that fixes the json rows. The honest fix is probably assert_representable: refuse a non-finite float on the write side, the way it already refuses an integer wider than int64, and say what to do instead (store the string). Reading is a separate question -- a type:float plaintext of +Inf / NaN is accepted by _deserialize_value today and should stay accepted, since Go writes it.

Not folded into #56: it moves wire behaviour (a croak where there was none) and wants its own ADR paragraph. Wire lane.
